  "title": "Ensign Targets 15 Rigs in Venezuela. Who Gets Paid as Drilling Expands?",
  "deck": "The Canadian contractor sees more work from Chevron and is negotiating with other customers. Its filings show why collecting payment matters as much as winning contracts.",
  "category": "investigations",
  "categoryLabel": "Investigations",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-21T13:55:21.522Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-21T13:55:21.522Z",
  "body": [
    "Ensign Energy Services wants to grow its Venezuela fleet from two operating drilling rigs to about 15 over the next two years, CEO Bob Geddes told Reuters on Nov. 20. The Canadian contractor has already moved a third rig from the Permian Basin and expects it to start work by year-end. Geddes said a fourth rig was on its way from Australia.",
    "The two operating rigs have been working for Chevron. Geddes said three more are slated for Chevron and that Ensign is in talks with other potential customers for the remaining ten in its target. Chevron did not immediately respond to Reuters about Ensign’s plans. The 15-rig figure is Ensign’s target for the fleet, and ten of the proposed additions still depend on customer negotiations.",
    "Ensign stands to earn drilling-service revenue as rigs go to work. Chevron, an oil producer, stands to gain if the extra drilling translates into higher output from its Venezuelan operations. In September, Chevron announced plans to invest more than US$7 billion over five years and target production of about 600,000 barrels a day, the Associated Press reported. The companies’ goals measure different things: one counts drilling rigs and the other counts barrels produced.",
    "Ensign’s quarterly report shows the gap between doing the work and getting paid. At Sept. 30, the company carried about C$9.2 million in net receivables for work already performed in Venezuela. It said it had a history of collecting payments there, while warning that political unrest and sanctions could affect collection. U.S. Treasury licenses authorize certain oil-sector transactions and services under specified conditions.",
    "Geddes told Reuters he expects the pull on North American rig capacity to push rates up. Ensign plans to upgrade and recertify rigs drawn from its Permian fleet before shipping them south. The next measures of the expansion are rigs entering service, additional customers signing contracts and Ensign collecting the resulting bills."
  ],
